WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.530
So I really get into
this, you know, sentence.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:08.700
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here and
today I want to talk about a theme

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.629
that I've discussed before and that
is the sort of combination of big

00:00:12.629 --> 00:00:16.980
picture learning and nuts and bolts
learning in, in language learning.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:20.640
So if you enjoy these videos,
please subscribe, click on

00:00:20.645 --> 00:00:22.020
the bell for notifications.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:26.159
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, which I recommend,

00:00:26.159 --> 00:00:29.490
especially if you're learning
English, uh, please leave a comment.

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:34.220
Uh, I should say that that, uh, the
ability to listen to interesting

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:37.519
content, whether it be in the
language you're learning, or even

00:00:37.519 --> 00:00:41.660
in content of interest to you in
your own language is so powerful.

00:00:41.665 --> 00:00:45.860
The quality of the audio, the range of
material that's out there right now,

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:50.620
I'm listening to an English language
book from Audible called American

00:00:50.625 --> 00:00:55.070
Psychosis, which traces the history
of, sort of, right wing hysterical

00:00:55.099 --> 00:00:56.420
movements in the United States.

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:57.680
And it's very interesting.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:02.205
So sometimes I take a break from
listening  to Arabic and I listen to

00:01:02.205 --> 00:01:03.765
the book while I'm doing the dishes.

00:01:04.364 --> 00:01:07.664
Now getting back to the subject
of big picture learning and

00:01:07.695 --> 00:01:09.104
nuts and bolts learning.

00:01:09.465 --> 00:01:12.164
So for me, Arabic is difficult.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:14.940
Uh, it's difficult for
a number of reasons.

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:17.729
I think, first of all, it's, it's
written in the Arabic script,

00:01:17.729 --> 00:01:19.710
which is still difficult for me.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:23.429
It has more complicated
grammar than Persian because

00:01:23.434 --> 00:01:24.720
it has masculine, feminine.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:30.060
It has, you know, one, you know, dual
like plural and then completely plural.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:34.850
It has a number of sort of somewhat
more finicky details of grammar

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:38.750
than say Persian, which is more
similar to what we are familiar to in

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:42.920
Indo-European languages and additional
difficulty with Arabic is that there

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.140
are different, uh, forms of Arabic.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:48.080
So there's Egyptian Arabic, there's
Lenvantine Arabic, and of course,

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:52.850
North African Arabic and, and Gulf
Arabic and Iraqi Arabic and Yemeni.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:55.380
I don't know, so many forms of a language.

00:01:56.190 --> 00:02:00.750
And because I like to access the language
and the culture in so many different

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:05.730
ways, then political discussions by and
large are gonna be in standard Arabic.

00:02:06.135 --> 00:02:11.295
But I found the MTV Lebanon, which
is also political discussion, but

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:13.995
it's in Lebanese or Levantine Arabic.

00:02:14.025 --> 00:02:19.305
Movies typically on Netflix are, most
of them are either in, uh, you know,

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:21.075
they're either from Lebanon or from Egypt.

00:02:21.405 --> 00:02:24.495
So I'm, I wanna have some
sense of those languages.

00:02:24.585 --> 00:02:28.005
So I'm really studying three
languages, Standard Arabic,

00:02:28.315 --> 00:02:30.555
Levantine Arabic and Egyptian Arabic.

00:02:30.585 --> 00:02:34.395
And I even got a book that compares
Levantine Arabic to Egyptian Arabic.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:38.520
Getting back to MTV Lebanon, which
is quite difficult, they're having

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:42.300
these political discussions and
there's, again with Arabic, there's

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.430
so much more vocabulary in Arabic.

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:46.650
It seems so much more.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.980
You need more vocabulary
than in Persian has been my

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:51.360
experience for whatever reason.

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:56.790
So it, it looks like, um,
constant uphill battle.

00:02:57.734 --> 00:03:00.285
And I'm wondering if I've ever,
if I'm actually getting anywhere.

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:04.515
So what I do is, so I've started
doing this now with the material

00:03:04.515 --> 00:03:09.075
from MTV Lebanon, which by the
way I download the MP3 file, I

00:03:09.075 --> 00:03:11.115
put the MP3 file on Happy Scribe.

00:03:11.415 --> 00:03:12.585
I get a transcript.

00:03:12.704 --> 00:03:15.675
I upload the whole thing
into LingQ as a lesson.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:18.225
Then I go through it in sentence mode.

00:03:18.555 --> 00:03:22.859
Now, right away by going in and
going through sentence mode I'm

00:03:22.859 --> 00:03:24.510
starting to slow things down.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.420
The, the big picture would be
just to listen to the whole thing,

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:31.320
read through the whole thing, not
understand as much as I would like.

00:03:31.325 --> 00:03:38.790
It seems like a, you know, a real uphill
struggle, Sisyphean task, so to speak.

00:03:39.149 --> 00:03:43.785
But if I go through sentence by sentence,
then I look up each word, I can then

00:03:43.845 --> 00:03:49.335
review, you know, have sort of vocabulary
review activities for that sentence,

00:03:49.485 --> 00:03:53.775
which could be three or four items
of vocabulary using matching pairs.

00:03:54.105 --> 00:03:55.965
Then I can reassemble the sentence.

00:03:56.235 --> 00:03:58.905
Also I can listen to
the audio several times.

00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:03.315
So I really get into
this, you know, sentence.

00:04:03.315 --> 00:04:06.810
Of course a sentence is
a sample of the language.

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:10.860
And so I don't do this the whole time,
but I'll do this for a fair amount of

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:15.810
the time, because if I can break, if I
can get into, if I can break the code of

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:20.400
the sentence, get a better sense of the
structure, get a better, especially now

00:04:20.405 --> 00:04:25.230
dealing with, you know, Lebanese dialect
and some of the words that they're using.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:30.180
So I get into some depth with each
sentence and I listen to it quite a few

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:35.775
times, but it would take, it takes a
long time to get through the 30 or 45

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:41.505
or 50 minutes of this, um, you know,
radio interview or television interview,

00:04:41.804 --> 00:04:45.674
uh, you know, going sentenced by
sentence, reviewing all the words, re

00:04:45.734 --> 00:04:47.565
you know, reassembling the sentences.

00:04:47.685 --> 00:04:51.135
So I don't do it all the way through,
but it's a sample it's it reminds me

00:04:51.135 --> 00:04:55.664
of when we were, we were making lumber
for a very large, uh, prefabricated

00:04:55.664 --> 00:04:57.494
house manufacturer in Japan.

00:04:57.834 --> 00:05:03.825
We had very strict quality standards,
knot size, you know, twist, bend, uh,

00:05:03.825 --> 00:05:08.295
you know, cracks in the wood, whatever
we had very, very strict standards.

00:05:08.535 --> 00:05:13.920
So we would, you know, take a hundred
pieces out of the line every hour and

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:18.660
just to check on the quality and that
sample representative sort of what was

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:22.920
in the wood that we were processing,
because presumably the wood that we were

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:28.470
using, the sort of raw material would've
been of a, you know, one sort of origin.

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:31.800
And so to some extent, the quality
of the hundred pieces that we

00:05:31.805 --> 00:05:36.015
took out was representative of
the quality of the whole run.

00:05:36.315 --> 00:05:40.635
And in the same way, I feel very
often if, if I can do this sort of

00:05:40.695 --> 00:05:46.215
in depth, nuts and bolts learning for
a sample percentage of this content,

00:05:46.365 --> 00:05:51.345
it helps me when I do the sort of big
picture listening to the whole thing.

00:05:51.715 --> 00:05:52.765
Reading the whole thing.

00:05:53.155 --> 00:05:58.284
Similarly, similarly, uh, as I'm going
through my, uh, you know, lesson,

00:05:58.284 --> 00:06:03.505
this, uh, television interview from
MTV Lebanon, it takes me a long time to

00:06:03.505 --> 00:06:07.255
look up every single word and to check
every single, single yellow word that

00:06:07.255 --> 00:06:08.995
is a word that I've looked up before.

00:06:09.745 --> 00:06:14.455
So at some point I say, okay, rather
than read through the whole thing, which

00:06:14.515 --> 00:06:18.885
for me, reading in Arabic is much slower
than reading in the Latin alphabet.

00:06:19.215 --> 00:06:23.385
So at some point maybe halfway
through, I start to skip.

00:06:23.445 --> 00:06:26.835
So I skip from blue word to yellow
word to yellow word to blue word.

00:06:26.835 --> 00:06:30.895
So really only looking up the words that
I either don't know because I haven't

00:06:30.914 --> 00:06:35.474
seen them before or that I'm still not
really that familiar with, even though

00:06:35.474 --> 00:06:37.875
I've seen them maybe 3, 4, 5, 6 times.

00:06:38.055 --> 00:06:39.375
So I look those up again.

00:06:39.435 --> 00:06:45.495
So I'm just jumping from unknown word,
not fully, you know, understood word

00:06:45.855 --> 00:06:47.355
and going through the text that way.

00:06:47.415 --> 00:06:48.105
I still...

00:06:48.105 --> 00:06:53.025
and so that's a kind of a nuts and
bolts sampling of the vocabulary, but

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:54.585
I still listen to the whole thing.

00:06:55.155 --> 00:07:01.065
And to some of the words that I, you know,
in my sampling looked up, some of those,

00:07:01.065 --> 00:07:05.085
I remember some of them, I notice when I
listen, but it's not a hundred percent.

00:07:05.474 --> 00:07:09.765
And so much of language learning is not
a hundred percent, but it's a sampling.

00:07:10.215 --> 00:07:14.055
So I can go through, you know,
sentence by sentence, reassembling

00:07:14.055 --> 00:07:17.265
sentences, getting a better grasp of
the structure of the language, getting

00:07:17.265 --> 00:07:20.969
a better grasp of some vocabulary
items, then I might speed up the

00:07:20.969 --> 00:07:23.099
sampling by skipping from word to word.

00:07:23.549 --> 00:07:25.770
Uh, but I still listen to the whole thing.

00:07:25.799 --> 00:07:30.270
So I'm combining nuts and bolts
with, you know, big picture learning.

00:07:30.659 --> 00:07:35.010
And I, I, I think, you know, this is
something I recommend that you find

00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:40.020
your own combination and your own
way of combining I, I should, while I

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:44.090
think of it, another form of nuts and
bolts learning is to look up grammar

00:07:44.090 --> 00:07:47.610
issues, things that you're, you know,
questions that you have in your mind

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:49.170
about why it works this way and that way.

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:51.120
And there's so many
resources for doing that.

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:55.650
So just to, to finish up here so
I, I think it's important to have a

00:07:55.650 --> 00:08:00.030
combination, have a strategy, your own
strategy, uh, where you combine the nuts

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:05.265
and bolts with the top down learning,
you still need to do a lot of top, like

00:08:05.265 --> 00:08:09.435
big picture learning, lots of reading,
lots of listening, but you also need to

00:08:09.435 --> 00:08:11.594
spend some time on the nuts and bolts.

00:08:11.685 --> 00:08:12.765
So there you go.

00:08:12.794 --> 00:08:13.815
Thank you for listening.

00:08:14.054 --> 00:08:14.745
Bye for now.

